WRITTEN ARGUMENTS ON BEHALF OF THE ACCUSED

RESPECTFULLY SUBMITTED:

I. CONSPIRACY EVIDENCE

The prosecution has failed to establish the ingredients of Section 120B BNS. There is no direct evidence of any agreement between the applicants to commit murder. The alleged conspiracy meeting is not supported by any independent witness.

II. CALL RECORDS

The mobile call records merely show communication between the applicants. This is normal for business partners. Communication alone does not establish criminal conspiracy. The content of the conversations is not known.

III. CIRCUMSTANTIAL EVIDENCE

The entire prosecution case rests on circumstantial evidence. However, the chain of circumstances is broken at multiple links. The circumstances do not unequivocally point to the guilt of the applicants.

IV. LACK OF EYE-WITNESS

There is no eye-witness to the murder. No one saw the applicants committing the offence. The prosecution relies entirely on inference and conjecture.

V. WEAPON RECOVERY

The recovery of the weapon is suspicious. There were no independent witnesses to the recovery. The seizure memo was prepared in a manner that raises doubts about its authenticity.

VI. DNA EVIDENCE

The DNA evidence is affected by chain of custody issues. The samples were not properly preserved, and there are gaps in the documentation. This affects the reliability of the DNA evidence.

VII. DESTRUCTION OF EVIDENCE

The allegation of destruction of evidence under Section 201 BNS is not supported by any direct evidence. No one saw the applicants destroying any evidence. This is a mere allegation without proof.

VIII. MOTIVE

Business rivalry is a common phenomenon and does not establish motive for murder. Many businesses have rivalries without any criminal incidents. The motive alleged is weak and insufficient.

IX. BENEFIT OF DOUBT

When the prosecution evidence is weak and circumstantial, the benefit of doubt must go to the accused. The prosecution has failed to prove the case beyond reasonable doubt.

PRAYER

It is therefore prayed that the applicants be discharged/acquitted as the case may be.

